  "id": "gendai_hogaku",
  "name": "Gendai hōgaku (contemporary music for Japanese instruments)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Gendai hōgaku is newly composed concert music for Japanese instruments that grew after the Second World War, as composers wrote for shakuhachi, koto, shamisen, biwa and percussion in mixed ensembles. Nihon Ongaku Shūdan, known abroad as Pro Musica Nipponia and founded in Tokyo in 1964 with the composer Miki Minoru as artistic director, made it a touring and recording repertoire, often using the seventeen-string bass koto.",
